.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library implements the Simple Two-way Active Measurement Protocol
(STAMP, RFC 8762) with the Session-Sender Identifier of RFC 8972:
its packets in unauthenticated mode, its timestamps and error
estimate, the Session-Reflector's core, the Session-Sender's core and
the UDP socket of either end.
It has no other dependency than the C library.
The authenticated mode
.Pq Dv STAMP_AUTH , No 112 octets and an HMAC
is not implemented yet; the TLVs that follow the base packet are
read by
.Fn stamp_tlv_next
and otherwise carried as they are.
.Ss Packets
.Fn stamp_test_encode
writes a Session-Sender test packet
.Pq Vt struct stamp_test : No sequence number, timestamp, error estimate, SSID
into the first
.Dv STAMP_UNAUTH_LEN
(44) octets of
.Fa buf ,
its MBZ octets zero, what follows them as it is;
.Fa len
must be at least 44.
.Fn stamp_test_decode
reads one;
.Fn stamp_reflected_decode
reads a Session-Reflector's
.Pq Vt struct stamp_reflected ,
the sender's fields it echoes among them and whether its MBZ octets
were zero.
Each timestamp is read in the format its error estimate's Z bit says.
.Fn stamp_tlv_next
returns the TLV at
.Fa *off
(from the end of the base packet) and moves
.Fa *off
past it: 1, 0 when none is left, or \-1 with
.Va errno
.Er EBADMSG
when one runs past the packet.
.Ss Timestamps and the error estimate
.Fn stamp_ntp_put
and
.Fn stamp_ntp_get
convert a
.Vt timespec
of
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME
to and from NTP's 64-bit format (RFC 5905), its era told by the top
bit of the seconds as RFC 4330 does;
.Fn stamp_ptp_put
and
.Fn stamp_ptp_get ,
PTPv2's truncated format (seconds and nanoseconds), whose epoch is
the caller's to choose.
.Fn stamp_ts_put
and
.Fn stamp_ts_get
choose by an error estimate's
.Dv STAMP_EE_Z
bit.
.Fn stamp_ts_sub
is
.Fa a
\-
.Fa b
in seconds.
.Pp
.Fn stamp_ee_make
makes an error estimate (RFC 4656 section 4.1.2) of
.Fa err
seconds: the S bit when
.Fa synced ,
Z when
.Fa ptp ,
the least scale whose multiplier fits an octet, the multiplier
rounded up and never 0.
.Fn stamp_ee_seconds
is the error an estimate says.
.Fn stamp_ee_clock
is the clock's, from
.Xr ntp_gettime 2 :
synchronised unless its state is
.Dv TIME_ERROR ,
the error its maximum error.
.Ss The Session-Reflector
.Fn stamp_refl_new
makes a reflector of
.Vt struct stamp_refl_conf :
.Bl -tag -width rate_total
.It Va stateful
The reply's sequence number counts the packets of its session (RFC 8762
section 4.3.2) rather than copying the sender's; a session is the
caller's tag (a FIB), the sender's address and port, the address the
packet came to and the SSID.
.It Va rate , burst
Replies a second to one source address, and the burst its token bucket
allows (0: twice the rate); a rate of 0 sets no limit.
The buckets are a table of 4096, four ways a set, keyed by a random
secret, a new source taking its set's oldest.
.It Va rate_total
Replies a second to all sources, its burst as many; 0 none.
.It Va sess_max , sess_idle
Sessions kept at most
.Pq Dv STAMP_SESS_MAX , No 4096 , No the oldest replaced
and the seconds one ends after without a packet
.Pq Dv STAMP_SESS_IDLE , No 300 ;
0 for those.
.It Va port
The reflector's own port: a packet from it is not answered, nor one
from port 0.
.El
.Pp
.Fn stamp_refl_setconf
changes it, its sessions kept while it stays stateful.
.Fn stamp_refl_reply
answers a packet as it came
.Pq Vt struct stamp_rx : No the packet, the sender's address, the address it came to, the TTL it came with, the time it came Pq T2 ,
the session's tag and a monotonic time (0: now) into
.Fa out
and returns the reply's length, always the packet's: what follows the
base is copied.
A packet shorter than the base, from port 0 or the reflector's own, or
over a rate limit is not answered: \-1, why in
.Fa *why
.Pq Dv STAMP_DROP_SHORT , STAMP_DROP_PORT , STAMP_DROP_RATE ,
counted.
The caller sets the reply's own timestamp (T3) as late as it can with
.Fn stamp_refl_send_time
and sends it.
.Fn stamp_refl_expire
ends the sessions idle for
.Va sess_idle ;
.Fn stamp_refl_stats
says the packets answered, those dropped by reason and the sessions.
.Ss The Session-Sender
.Fn stamp_sender_new
makes a sender of
.Vt struct stamp_sender_conf ,
one session to one reflector:
.Bl -tag -width timeout
.It Va ssid
The Session-Sender Identifier its packets carry and its replies must.
.It Va seq_set , seq0
The first sequence number when
.Va seq_set ;
else a random one, never 0, so that the first reply tells a stateful
reflector (whose new session counts from 0) from a stateless one.
.It Va mode
The reflector's mode when known
.Pq Dv STAMP_MODE_STATELESS , STAMP_MODE_STATEFUL ;
.Dv STAMP_MODE_UNKNOWN
for the first reply to tell.
.It Va timeout
Seconds a reply is waited for (0: 2); one later is lost.
.It Va window
Packets in flight at most (0: 256).
.El
.Pp
Like the reflector's, the core does no I/O: the caller sends and
receives, each time on the monotonic clock
.Pq Fn stamp_mono , No or Dv STAMP_SOCK_MONOTONIC Ns 's receive time .
.Fn stamp_sender_packet
writes the next test packet into
.Fa buf ,
its timestamp
.Fa t1
(the time of day), sent at
.Fa mono ;
it returns 0, or 1 when the window was full and its oldest packet
given up, lost, that one's sample in
.Fa *lostp .
.Fn stamp_sender_reply
matches a reply that came at
.Fa mono
to its packet by the SSID and the sender's sequence number it echoes:
1 with its sample in
.Fa *out ,
0 for none of the session's packets in flight (another SSID, a reply
late or again), \-1 for no reflected packet.
A sample
.Pq Vt struct stamp_sample
has the round trip with the reflector's time taken out,
.Va rtt
= (T4 \- T1) \- (T3 \- T2), T1 and T4 monotonic and T2 and T3 both the
reflector's, so that neither clock need be right;
.Va refl
is T3 \- T2, and
.Va r
the reply.
.Fn stamp_sender_expire
gives up the oldest packet in flight past its timeout: 1 with its
sample
.Pq Va lost ,
0 when there is none; it is called until 0.
.Fn stamp_sender_next
is when the oldest packet in flight times out, 0 with none.
.Pp
.Fn stamp_sender_stats
counts
.Pq Vt struct stamp_sender_stats
the packets sent, received and lost, the replies late (after their
timeout, or again) and foreign, and the reflector's mode, told by its
first reply.
A stateful reflector's sequence number counts what reached it, so
the losses between two replies are told apart:
.Va fwd_lost
never reached the reflector,
.Va back_lost
were reflected but not back in time.
A stateless reflector's are the round trip's alone,
.Va unknown_lost ,
as are those before the first reply and those across a counter that
went back or leapt (the reflector started again).
The losses between two replies are counted once every packet before
the later one is answered or lost, up to a timeout after; those after
the last reply are in
.Va lost
alone.
.Fn stamp_mode_name
names a mode.
.Ss Sockets
.Fn stamp_socket
opens a non-blocking UDP socket of family
.Fa af ,
bound to
.Fa port
(0: any) on every address, an IPv6 one
.Dv IPV6_V6ONLY ,
in the process's FIB
.Pq Xr setfib 2 ;
the time a packet comes is the kernel's
.Pq Dv SO_TIMESTAMP , SO_TS_REALTIME ,
the TTL or hop limit and the address it came to said.
.Dv STAMP_SOCK_REFLECTOR
in
.Fa flags
sends with TTL 255;
.Dv STAMP_SOCK_MONOTONIC
has the kernel's receive time on the monotonic clock
.Pq Dv SO_TS_MONOTONIC ,
a sender's.
.Fn stamp_socket_fib
opens it in FIB
.Fa fib
.Pq Dv SO_SETFIB , No before it is bound ;
\-1 for the process's.
.Fn stamp_recv
reads a packet and what came with it
.Pq Vt struct stamp_rxinfo :
.Va t
the time of day it came,
.Va mono
the monotonic time, each the kernel's when it said, else the
clock's.
.Fn stamp_send
sends from the address
.Fa from
(the one a packet came to), the routes' own when that is not of an
interface of the socket's FIB.
.Fn stamp_sockaddr
resolves a host and port;
.Fn stamp_sa_port
and
.Fn stamp_sa_ntop
read a socket address.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The functions returning
.Vt int
or
.Vt ssize_t
return \-1 with
.Va errno
set on failure;
.Fn stamp_refl_new
and
.Fn stamp_sender_new
return
.Dv NULL .
